    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Xanthomonas euvesicatoria (Xcv) is the causal agent of bacterial spot disease in pepper and tomato plants. Xcv uses its type III secretion (T3S) system to translocate effector proteins into the cytoplasm of plant cells. Although the function of each T3S effector is not well understood, it is known that they are required to collectively suppress innate immune signaling in plants.  In this thesis, I focused on one effector protein, XopX, which has been found to be a major contributor towards Xcv virulence. Using the yeast two-hybrid system and in vitro affinity protein purification, the Mudgett laboratory discovered that XopX physically associates with the Arabidopsis thaliana J-domain protein, J3. Given this finding, I hypothesize that XopX may be directly targeting J3 to alter its function during Xcv infection to promote pathogenesis. Here I studied the role of J3 in plant pathogen-triggered immunity (PTI) and effector-triggered immunity (ETI) by utilizing the heterologous Pseudomonas syringae pathovar tomato (Pst)-Arabidopsis thaliana pathosystem. Arabidopsis j3 mutants were less susceptible to Pst infection than wild-type Arabidopsis lines in PTI assays. By contrast, J3 did not appear to play a significant role in ETI. Studies examining the role of XopX in bacterial virulence in Arabidopsis revealed that both wild-type and j3 mutant leaves were equally more resistant to Pst expressing XopX compared to infection with Pst alone.   This suggests that XopX may be triggering Arabidopsis immune responses, possibly in a J3-dependent manner, or that XopX may be interfering with Pst virulence mechanisms during infection. In conclusion, my research identifies J3 as a negative regulator of PTI in Arabidopsis. It is unclear if XopX is directly interfering with J3 function; however, my preliminary findings suggest that J3 is required for XopX-induced resistance in Arabidopsis.",
